---
import ConverterPage from '../components/ConverterPage.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is Mangal a different encoding from Unicode?',
    a: 'No, and this is the single most useful thing to know here. Mangal is a <em>font</em>, not an encoding. It is an OpenType Unicode font that Microsoft ships with Windows. So "Kruti Dev to Mangal" means "Kruti Dev to Unicode, then displayed in Mangal". The text this page produces is ordinary Unicode Hindi and will work in any Unicode font.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do government forms specifically ask for Mangal?',
    a: 'Because Mangal was the first Devanagari font guaranteed to be present on every Windows machine, so specifying it meant a form would render the same way in every office. Many departmental circulars still name it by habit. In practice any Unicode Devanagari font (Mangal, Nirmala UI, Noto Sans Devanagari) displays the same characters.',
  },
  {
    q: 'The output panel does not look like Mangal on my device.',
    a: 'The panel asks for Mangal first and falls back to another Devanagari font if it is not installed, which is normal on Mac, Linux, Android and iOS, where Mangal does not ship. The characters are identical either way. On Windows it will render in genuine Mangal.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I get the converted text into a Word file as Mangal?',
    a: 'Use the <strong>Download .docx</strong> button. The file is written with Mangal already applied to every paragraph. Or copy the text, paste it into Word, select it and choose Mangal from the font list.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the matra order handled correctly?',
    a: 'Yes. The short-i sign is moved after its consonant, the reph is moved to the front of its cluster, split vowel signs are recomposed and the conjuncts क्ष, त्र, ज्ञ and श्र are mapped explicitly. This is the part most converters get wrong, and it is why text from elsewhere sometimes fails search or spell-check even though it looks fine.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I go the other way?',
    a: 'Yes. See the <a href="/mangal-to-kruti-dev">Mangal to Kruti Dev converter</a>. There is a link to it under the boxes on this page too.',
  },
];
---

<ConverterPage
  path="/kruti-dev-to-mangal"
  lede="Paste your Kruti Dev text on the left and get Mangal-ready Unicode Hindi on the right, previewed in real Mangal. For government forms, NPS and CRA portals."
  faq={faq}
>
  <h2>First, the thing nobody tells you</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Mangal is a font, not an encoding.</strong> It is the Unicode
    Devanagari font that Microsoft has shipped with Windows since XP. Kruti Dev,
    by contrast, is a legacy font that maps Hindi onto ASCII slots, which is
    why a Kruti Dev document looks like <code>Hkkjr</code> on a computer that
    does not have it.
  </p>
  <p>
    So "Kruti Dev to Mangal" is really "Kruti Dev to Unicode, shown in Mangal".
    The text this page gives you is standard Unicode Hindi. Paste it into a
    form, a portal, an email or a database and it will be correct regardless
    of which Devanagari font ends up rendering it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why your department asks for Mangal</h2>
  <p>
    When Unicode Hindi first became practical in government work, Mangal was the
    only Devanagari font you could rely on being installed on every Windows
    machine. Specifying it in a circular was a way of guaranteeing that a form
    would look the same in every office. That instruction has outlived its
    reason, because Nirmala UI and Noto Sans Devanagari render the same text
    just as well, but the wording survives in departmental guidance, which is why
    people search for "Kruti Dev to Mangal" rather than "to Unicode".
  </p>
  <p>
    If a portal rejects your text, the problem is almost never the font. It is
    usually that the text was pasted as Kruti Dev ASCII rather than converted,
    or that the matra order is wrong, which is precisely what this converter
    fixes.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting it into a document as Mangal</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Paste your Kruti Dev text on the left. The Mangal output appears as you
      type.
    </li>
    <li>
      Press <strong>Download .docx</strong> for a Word file with Mangal already
      applied, or <strong>Copy text</strong> to paste it yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      If you paste manually into Word, select the text and pick
      <strong>Mangal</strong> from the font list. Setting the font does not
      change the characters, it only changes how they are drawn.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Mangal, Nirmala UI or Noto?</h2>
  <p>
    Mangal is the oldest and has the most conservative letterforms, which is why
    it still reads as the "official" choice. Nirmala UI, introduced with Windows
    8, is cleaner on screen. Noto Sans Devanagari is Google's open font and is
    what this site uses for its own Hindi text. All three display identical
    Unicode, so pick whichever your form asks for, and do not worry that the
    underlying text changes. It does not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Need plain Unicode without the Mangal framing? Use the
    <a href="/">Kruti Dev to Unicode converter</a>. Going the other way? Try
    <a href="/mangal-to-kruti-dev">Mangal to Kruti Dev</a>.
  </p>
</ConverterPage>
